#lang at-exp racket/base

(require ycao-net/lib/markup)

(define post
  @article["AI, Intellectual Work, Humans, and the Future" "2026-10-27"]{
    @para{
      Sorry, this is another blog post about LLMs. It has actually only been half a year since the previous post @link["/posts/llm-after-three-years.html"]{"Three Years with LLMs"}, but so much has indeed happened in that half year, mainly OpenAI's NS blow-up solution, more than seven hundred mathematical proofs, and @link["https://openai.com/index/building-scientific-understanding-with-academic-communities/"]{the solutions to three extremely difficult problems, released today}. It is clearly no longer possible to assume that LLMs are just a souped-up search engine or are taking the convex hull of human knowledge. What can now be asserted is that for formal, a priori problems, LLMs can greatly help (help! after all, ordinary people using astra prompts clearly cannot produce results—I tried feeding the Lean-written Infinitely Many Mersenne Primes Conj to astra, and after trying for an hour it gave up. But mathematicians clearly benefit a great deal from AI) human exploration and achieve results that were previously out of reach. This is still quite some distance from AGI, but it is enough to overturn previous assumptions and make some new judgments.
    }
    @para{
      Will LLMs encounter bottlenecks? I don't know... I can only say that new evidence shows the scaling law is still effective, and people at the industry frontier have not seen obvious obstacles. Standing in the middle of an exponentially developing curve, it would be surprising if it suddenly stopped. @cite["stanfordhai2026ConferencePhysics2026"] Interestingly, the latest wave of progress is quite closely connected to the development and use of Lean 4. Prior Art includes attempts to solve formal mathematical problems using purely a priori (Tabula Rasa) RL, but they all ended without practical value. @cite["wuTacticZeroLearningProve2021"] Many of the problems stem from rewards being too sparse, the solution space being too large, and training being unable to scale. It is reasonable to conjecture that LLMs learned human mathematics, used it as a starting point, and successfully bootstrapped mathematical RL with Lean. @cite["hubertOlympiadlevelFormalMathematical2026"] The impact of LLMs on other disciplines is not yet clear, but anything close to theory/formalization clearly cannot escape their reach.
    }
    @para{
      AI safety? This is indeed something that can be discussed seriously now. If we look at traditional security, @link["https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/vulnerability-discovery-and-exploitation-trends-in-the-ai-era"]{the number of CVEs in 2026 reached an unprecedented level}. But I am not too worried about this; after all, red teams are part of cybersecurity, and they have found so many CVEs—that's a good thing. Given that AI can also help with formal verification, future software may even finally have the resources to produce correctness proofs (for preliminary attempts, see @link["https://github.com/kim-em/lean-zip"]{lean-zip} and @link["https://www.anthropic.com/research/formal-verification"]{OSS-Fuzz}). (Formalization still has some pitfalls at present, such as @link["https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/zHhGa3iQBQcdSifFL/a-critical-look-at-lean4-1"]{Lean 4's soundness}, and Trusting Trust@cite["thompsonReflectionsTrustingTrust1984"], but acceptable solutions exist for all of these.)
      @img["/files/20261026/good-thing-meme.jpg" #:scale 0.3]{This is a good thing}
    }
    @para{
      Another AI safety issue worth considering is Reward Hacking, which is often discussed on LessWrong as AI doom. Famous thought experiments include @link["https://www.lesswrong.com/w/paperclip-maximizer"]{the paperclip factory}. I have indeed encountered it myself: when testing Command Code, I encountered a bug and let the AI solve it on its own. The method consistent with human moral standards would have been to search online for documentation and the issue tracker, but the AI directly reverse-engineered this closed-source software. It did accomplish my goal, but the method it used was unethical. Setting aside my personal grudge against the pathological, hypocritical Silicon Valley culture on LessWrong, I am not overly worried about this either: since AI is so proficient at formalization, we can formalize some quite strong conditions, such as “any AI has a method for being forcibly shut down” or “a completely side-effect-free sandbox” or “any operation can be fully reversed within finite time”; these are clearly much easier to formalize than “do not harm humans.” More obvious solutions also include human imitation and the like. If one analyzes interests and harms purely formally, it would be strange if no problems emerged—after all, this stuff (moral philosophy) is full of contradictions, ambiguity, and relativism. Of course, this article does not aspire to solve AI safety, especially since research on it is still quite early.
    }
    @para{
      Human value... what a painful topic. We have always considered ourselves unique because of intelligence, standing above all species. But the era we are now entering depicts a prospect just like “The Evolution of Human Science”@cite["chiangTheEvolutionHumanScience2016"], with scientists picking over AI's leftovers. Is this depressing...? For most people, there is always someone with higher intelligence (assuming intelligence is ordered) than you, but you have never felt depressed because of that. I once agonized over this; ultimately it comes down to education having instilled too much meritocracy and utilitarianism—why must you be better than a person (now a thing)—usually intellectually better—in order to be happy? If it makes you feel a little better, a rather insightful friend of mine pointed out that from a cognitive science perspective, “How can humans be so stupid and yet accomplish so much? It is already remarkable.” Indeed, the Homo sapiens brain has for the vast majority of time optimized for socializing and hunting@cite["dunbarSocialBrainHypothesis1998"]; from this perspective, civilization's pushing formal answer search to the modern level in such a short time is almost unimaginable@cite["dehaeneCulturalRecyclingCortical2007"]. Losing to a machine is not shameful.
      (However, this article will not delve deeply into human value here (nihilism? existentialism?); I myself am still quite lacking in philosophical literacy.)
    }
    @para{
      On the contrary, what deserves more attention is social value and distribution. The Industrial Revolution reorganized traditional modes of labor, and the gains from increased productivity did not immediately benefit ordinary workers. Later, so-called intellectual workers—at the top entrepreneurs and politicians, then researchers, programmers, and so on—stood on the upper and middle rungs of the social ladder. Now these mental workers have also tasted what laid-off workers in the nineteenth century experienced@cite["allenEngelsPauseTechnical2009"]. Continuing from “Three Years”, we (especially China) must realize that intelligence should not be characterized as the standard for measuring human value—in fact, there should not be any standard; the utilitarian value of this standard is almost over. Each day, a person needs to take in two thousand kilocalories, avoid dying of illness, and the rest is just doing things they want to do; the pathological social evaluation system will eventually be abandoned. Economic distribution is even more urgent. It is obvious that AI can improve people's lives: we might as well construct a trivial solution—keep all existing relations of production unchanged; after AI replaces some jobs, everyone stays in their original positions, spending most of their time idling or on vacation, and the saved costs are enough to cover AI's operating costs. This is strictly better than the era without AI, but in reality the stock market keeps climbing, while people's lives seem to be getting worse. This still comes back to the mismatch between relations of production and productive capacity; clearly, the -ism of a certain -ist country has not been functioning. Additionally, there are worries about the Final Owner—OpenAI and Anthropic already have quite pathological cultures and monopolistic tendencies. Fortunately, Chinese labs have been releasing open-source models, and their intelligence has no essential generational gap with frontier SOTA models, so I think the possibility of monopoly capital achieving a total victory is still quite small.
    }
    @blockquote{
      It was the best of times, it was the worst of times, it was the age of wisdom, it was the age of foolishness, it was the epoch of belief, it was the epoch of incredulity, it was the season of light, it was the season of darkness, it was the spring of hope, it was the winter of despair.
    }
  }
)

(provide post)